  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 AM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nConvective banding already starting to pick up on radar this\nmorning, with snow spreading across Jackson Hole and over\nTogwotee Pass currently. The northwest flow will continue\nproducing scattered snow with the leading edge of the lifting\naloft, but snowfall rates in the western mountains will be\nfairly low into this afternoon. Once the next trough and\nassociated jet max sweep in starting around sunset tonight,\nbetter lifting and moisture will result in quicker and more\nproductive snowfall production, and this activity will also \nspread into the Bighorns after sunset. The peak snowfall \nproduction will generally occur between midnight tonight and \nnoon Saturday. Overall snow amount forecast has not changed \nnoticeably, so current alignment of Winter Storm Warnings and \nAdvisories will not need adjustments with this package. \n\nAs the trough sweeps through overnight, an associated cold front\nwill race southward for areas east of the Divide, bringing\ncolder temperatures back into the region. As it pivots through,\na band of snow showers will sweep across east of the Divide, \nbringing a brief band of snowfall to the Bighorn and Wind River \nBasins and across Johnson and Natrona Counties. Since the system\nis moving through quickly, the period of snowfall will be \nbrief, but some accumulations are expected, ranging from a trace\nto one inch for most of the Wind River and Bighorn Basins, to \none to two inches for Johnson and Natrona Counties downwind from\nthe Bighorn Range. By noon or so on Saturday, the system will \nbe well to the southeast, and all snowfall production will be \ncomplete, both higher and lower elevation. Daytime highs will be\ndependent on how much snow stick to surfaces, but highs will \nstruggle to get into the 30s east of the Divide. Most of the \nGreen River Basin and Sweetwater County will miss out on this \nsystem, so highs will push back into the low to mid 40s on \nSaturday.\n\nBehind this system, broad H5 ridging return across the Rockies.\nTemperatures Sunday will again hover in the 30s for most areas,\nwith warmer temperatures along the I-80 corridor. The broad\ntrough off the West Coast will eject into Canada on Monday. The\ntail end of this system will clip western Wyoming, bringing\nanother round of snow to the western mountains. As flow from\nthis system will be more form the southwest, and moisture will\nbe more limited, snowfall amounts Monday will be low.\n\nThe general trend into next week will be temperatures near\nnormal, while the main storm track sets up farther to the south\nacross the Four Corners region.",
